## **10. Node library specification**

The first release should define these 45 nodes, grouped into nine categories.

| Category         | Nodes                                                                                |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Input (5)        | Load Image, Load Video, Text Prompt, Reference Image, Load 3D Scene                  |
| Segmentation (5) | Auto Segment, Point Segment, Box Segment, Text Select, Mask Refine                   |
| Editing (6)      | Inpaint, Outpaint, Object Remove, Object Insert, Material Replace, Relight           |
| Conditioning (6) | Depth Map, Canny Edge, Line Art, Normal Map, IPAdapter, Geometry Lock                |
| Architecture (6) | Building Type, Room Type, Material Palette, Camera Settings, Style Preset, Landscape |
| Environment (5)  | Weather, Climate, Sun Position, Mood, Atmosphere                                     |
| Generation (5)   | Text-to-Image, Image-to-Image, Upscale, Detail Enhance, Batch Variations             |
| Video (4)        | Image-to-Video, Camera Motion, Frame Interpolation, Video Export                     |
| Output (3)       | Image Preview, Compare Versions, Save Asset                                          |

### **Node execution contract**

Every node must have a stable identifier, version, typed input/output ports, parameter schema, validation rules, execution adapter, resource estimate, and error state.

Example architecture-specific edit node:

```

{
  "id": "edit_facade_01",
  "type": "archviz.masked_edit",
  "version": "1.0",
  "inputs": {
    "image": "asset://render_001",
    "mask": "asset://facade_mask",
    "reference": "asset://stone_reference"
  },
  "parameters": {
    "instruction": "Replace selected facade with travertine",
    "preserveGeometry": true,
    "referenceStrength": 0.75,
    "maskFeatherPx": 8,
    "seed": 12345
  },
  "outputs": {
    "image": "image",
    "differenceMap": "image"
  }
}

```

`preserveGeometry` is a requested constraint. The backend must translate it into available structural conditioning and quality checks rather than treating it as an absolute guarantee.

## **11. Core user stories and acceptance criteria**

| ID    | User story                                    | Acceptance criteria                                               |
| ----- | --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| US-01 | Generate an architectural image from a prompt | User receives an image and reproducible generation metadata       |
| US-02 | Edit one facade without changing the rest     | Masked generation, before/after review, geometry-change warning   |
| US-03 | Select windows automatically                  | Click/text selection returns an editable mask                     |
| US-04 | Apply a material reference                    | Reference is bound to a target region and recorded in history     |
| US-05 | Change sunny daytime to rainy dusk            | Weather and lighting settings are reflected in the output         |
| US-06 | Generate a camera-motion video                | Video job can be queued, previewed, and exported                  |
| US-07 | Reuse a successful workflow                   | Save, reopen, duplicate, and rerun a versioned graph              |
| US-08 | Run on local or cloud hardware                | Job routing reflects supported model and GPU requirements         |
| US-09 | Recover a failed generation                   | Failure reason is visible; retry does not corrupt project history |
| US-10 | Preserve approved design versions             | Original images remain immutable and revisions are traceable      |

## **12. Project data and API requirements**

The initial database entities should be `users`, `projects`, `assets`, `asset_versions`, `workflows`, `workflow_versions`, `nodes`, `edges`, `masks`, `annotations`, `reference_sets`, `presets`, `generation_jobs`, `model_registry`, and `audit_events`.

Core endpoints:

```

POST /api/v1/projects
POST /api/v1/assets
POST /api/v1/segmentation
POST /api/v1/workflows/validate
POST /api/v1/workflows/compile
POST /api/v1/jobs
GET  /api/v1/jobs/{job_id}
POST /api/v1/jobs/{job_id}/cancel
GET  /api/v1/projects/{id}/versions
GET  /api/v1/models/capabilities
POST /api/v1/videos/render

```

Use WebSockets or server-sent events for queue progress and preview updates. Store original assets and approved revisions immutably. Mask coordinates must remain correctly aligned across image resizing and cropping.

Security requirements include authenticated project access, signed asset URLs, upload validation, model isolation, per-user GPU quotas, audit logs, and explicit consent controls for cloud processing.

## **13. Development roadmap**

1. **Phase 1 — Studio foundation**

   Weeks 1–3

   Build the React application, node editor, project management, image upload, graph validation, and ComfyUI connection.

   **Deliverable:** User can generate an architectural image through a saved node workflow.
2. **Phase 2 — AI Canvas and segmentation**

   Weeks 4–7

   Add canvas layers, SAM2 integration, brush masks, annotations, reference-image inputs, masked inpainting, and version comparison.

   **Deliverable:** User can select and modify a facade or interior element.
3. **Phase 3 — Architecture intelligence**

   Weeks 8–10

   Add architecture presets, material library, ControlNet conditioning, mood, climate, lighting, camera presets, and geometry-difference checks.

   **Deliverable:** User can create controlled architectural variations with reusable presets.
4. **Phase 4 — Image-to-video**

   Weeks 11–13

   Integrate video models, camera-motion controls, generation queue, preview player, and MP4 export.

   **Deliverable:** Approved still renders can be animated and exported.
5. **Phase 5 — Production hardening**

   Weeks 14–16

   Add hybrid GPU routing, authentication, resource limits, testing, telemetry, backups, recovery, packaging, and licensing review.

   **Deliverable:** Deployable beta for Windows and cloud.

Indicative 16-week schedule for a small experienced engineering team, assuming reusable open-source components. It is not a fixed delivery commitment.

## **14. Local GPU and cloud deployment**

For your Windows 11 development environment with an RTX 3060 6 GB VRAM and 16 GB RAM, use the local machine primarily for frontend development, segmentation, lightweight image models, and reduced-resolution previews.

| Workload                                | Recommended execution                    |
| --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| Node editor and Canvas                  | Local                                    |
| SAM segmentation                        | Local, with memory optimization          |
| Lightweight image generation            | Local, model-dependent                   |
| SDXL / larger image models              | Cloud preferred for speed and resolution |
| High-resolution architectural rendering | Cloud GPU                                |
| Advanced image-to-video                 | Cloud GPU                                |
| Blender geometry processing             | Local or cloud                           |
| Production batch generation             | Cloud GPU workers                        |

Wan 2.2's documented 5B workflow targets approximately 8 GB VRAM with offloading, already above the 6 GB available on your GPU. Treat local video inference as experimental and route production video jobs to cloud hardware.&#x20;

